Thank you for that historical answer, Gleki.
Yes, I perfectly understand the huge damage it can cause.

Nevertheless, I also understand why this may arise: I *never *saw a set of=
=20
rules accepted by 100% of people!

   - Laws? There are plenty of debates. Countries have different ones.=20
   People disagree on many, but also often agree on *core* ones.
   - Coding style rules? There are as many guidelines as coders! However,=
=20
   enforcing the whole developer team to use them makes teamwork more=20
   effective. They're like a common *language*.
   - Language rules? Phew! Everyone knows someone who has his/her "very=20
   own" way to spell words, or organize sentences...
  =20
For the sake of Lojban's survival and spreading (and despite my very short=
=20
history on that language), I think all must agree on some *unique* version,=
=20
*and* accept the debate to make it evolve, *and* accept that not all=20
proposals will fit in, *and* that reintegration of proposals may take=20
months or years.
Said otherwise, we must all "agree to disagree", may I say...

By the way, your answer makes me think about software distributions. Do we=
=20
want our future GNU-Linux / Android / iOS / Windows / BSD / whatever=20
contain a lot of "jbo_*" (imagine "jbo_FR", "jbo_EN", "jbo_CA"...), or just=
=20
a unique "jbo" language?
Unicity of Lojban is, IMHO, vital. I personally would just drop learning=20
Lojban if I had no hope of that.

Thanks again.

la .sykyndyr.




